    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Tyler Soderstrom** (76.91) and **Mickey Moniak** (75.75) represent our most efficient starts, both heavily benefiting from a high-upside matchup against favorable opposing arms. **Tyler Soderstrom** exploits a +18% SP Skill bump against Lowder, while **Mickey Moniak** is in an ideal spot against Nick Martinez, fueled by a massive +14% Park factor and an equally favorable platoon advantage. **Seiya Suzuki** also warrants a high-confidence start (73.90) given the vulnerable 5.20 xERA of his opponent, confirming that our heavy hitters are well-positioned to capitalize on these soft matchups. **Corbin Carroll** remains an essential start (68.61) despite a slight drag from Mize\u2019s ability to hold runners; his elite speed and lead-off spot make him non-negotiable for our pursuit of SB category dominance.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today was excluding **Cole Young**, who fell just short of the threshold with a 37.96 score. While his potential lead-off spot provided a +10% bump, the combination of a tough Drew Anderson matchup (-19% SP Skill) and a park factor penalty forced him to the bench in favor of the higher-ceiling utility options. Conversely, we are keeping a close watch on **Austin Riley** (52.51); he is currently holding the 3B spot, but his status is heavily tethered to the 33% rain risk. If the weather reports deteriorate, we may need to pivot. We are also monitoring the lineup status of **Gabriel Moreno**, **Josh Naylor**, **Travis Bazzana**, **Max Muncy**, **Brice Turang**, **Henry Bolte**, and **Jose Caballero**, as their projected efficiency relies heavily on their confirmed order in the batting lineup. With **James Wood** and **Vinnie Pasquantino** safely tucked away on the IL, our active roster depth\u2014including the resting **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Trevor Story**, **Isaac Paredes**, **Dalton Rushing**, and **Edwin Arroyo**\u2014remains prepared for tactical swaps should any of our starters be scratched.",
